WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.516 --> 00:00:09.140
MIKE DUGGAN: My name is Michael Duggan.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:11.720
I'm a person in long-term recovery.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:17.180
What that means to me is I haven't had a drink or drug since April 14, 2009.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:21.960
My introduction to opioids began when I broke my wrist senior year

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:23.210
playing hockey in high school.

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.170
Before that, I had experimented with alcohol and marijuana.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:31.050
That also plays a part in my story that I think is important.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:32.890
I was a three sport varsity athlete.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.860
I loved playing sports, kept me out of a lot of trouble.

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.250
Senior year in high school, we were playing Hingham, Massachusetts.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.350
And it was about the second game into the season.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:47.300
I went to go check somebody and broke my wrist, shattered my wrist completely.

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:52.430
Went to the emergency room and was given a prescription of opioids

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:56.480
at the time to manage the pain.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.090
Looking back at it now, there was no lesser alternatives that were given.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.860
There was no questions, no family history that was drawn.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.820
It was pretty much the immediate solution.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:10.220
Because of my injury, I had nerve damage,

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.720
and I lost feeling in my fingertips.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:15.500
So I had to go back and get surgery on my wrist

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:17.750
and also get the nerve damage repaired.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:20.810
And the solution after that was another prescription

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:23.150
for opioid pain medication.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:28.244
So there was a lot of access to the drugs initially from the doctors.

00:01:28.244 --> 00:01:30.410
And I didn't really understand the immediate dangers

00:01:30.410 --> 00:01:34.270
to these drugs at all.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.070
And because of the injury, I was out of sports.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.540
So I had a lot of time now on my hands at the end of my senior year of class.

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.820
And I was kind of getting to the end of school, so more spent kind

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.700
of partying at that time in my life.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:54.460
And with the prescription opioid pain medication,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:57.150
it seemed like there was an abundance of these drugs everywhere.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:00.450
I had a lot of friends that also had injuries

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.070
that were being prescribed medication.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:05.700
It just seemed like it was easier for my generation,

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:09.600
as an underage person, to get access to illegal prescription

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.990
narcotics on the streets than to get somebody to buy you alcohol,

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.690
because of the accessibility of these drugs.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:18.570
And when you hear heroin, heroin has such a name branding

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.760
effect that terrifies a lot of people.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:27.910
But when you hear things like Percocet or 30s or OxyContin or 80s or jams,

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:29.310
it didn't have that same effect.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:33.660
I mean, we really didn't understand the seriousness of these drugs

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.600
and how they would completely take over your life once

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.700
becoming addicted to them.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:43.540
So eventually, at first, I didn't know that I had a problem to them.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:48.900
I didn't know I was addicted to them because I had a steady supply that

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.490
was being given to me.

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:55.020
And then I remember I went away on vacation that summer,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:03:01.230
and my girlfriend's family had a house in Old Orchard Beach in Maine.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.736
And she invited us up there for vacation for the weekend.

00:03:04.736 --> 00:03:05.610
And we went up there.

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:08.200
And I forgot to bring something with me.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.950
And I remember the first night, I was just deathly ill in withdrawal.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:15.510
It was the first time I ever felt any sickness like this.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:20.100
I was-- hot sweats, just crawling out of my skin,

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:24.150
tossing and turning, just completely restless and irritable.

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:28.210
And I knew that it was because of the drugs.

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:31.550
So the entire time as I'm feeling these physical symptoms there's also

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:35.730
a mental obsession that I need to get something quickly to make myself

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:37.350
feel better again.

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.800
And the next morning everybody went to the beach.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.770
And I told her family I didn't feel good, I had the flu.

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.060
And they believed me, of course, because the last thing they were going to think

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.760
was Mike's addicted to drugs, you know?

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:54.150
We have this image of what a drug addict is,

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.690
but the reality is there's no such thing.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:04:00.030
Addiction doesn't discriminate.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.050
Doesn't matter who you are, where you come from, what you look like.

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:08.310
The reality of the situation was that I was very much indeed addicted

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:09.750
to these drugs.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.160
So I left, and I went home.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.120
And I met up with somebody, and I bought one of these pills off the streets

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:15.810
illegally.

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:19.269
And I felt immediately better.

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:22.120
And at that moment in my life I made a decision

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.800
that I would never go anywhere again unless I bring something with me.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.750
And that's a real sad realization for a young 18-year-old kid

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:33.480
to make in his life, that he's such a prisoner to these drugs

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.290
that he can't even go away on vacation without taking them.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.780
So what happened in my life at that point

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:42.870
is I honestly went from a period of having fun

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.200
by taking these drugs to living in fear of withdrawal.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.240
So now, ultimately, my decisions were all based off of fear.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.340
And the only way for me to go to school and go to practice or go to work

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.336
was I needed to take these drugs to just feel normal.

00:04:59.336 --> 00:05:01.210
So it wasn't even the thrill of getting high.

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:06.005
It was just to feel normal on a daily basis, to function normally in society.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:12.990
It's just really sad to spiral out of control at that point for me.

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:15.730
The prescription lasted a lot longer than the pain itself.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.700
And I think that's very important.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:21.330
There was lesser alternatives that should have been given.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.910
The reality is, my family, there's a lot of history

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.729
with addiction and alcoholism that wasn't looked at,

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:29.520
that should have made me more of a high-risk candidate for somebody

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.060
who was being prescribed prescription opioid pain medication.

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:37.110
Back when my parents grew up, even my grandparents grew up, if you broke

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.450
your wrist you were given Tylenol.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:40.950
You were sent home with Motrin.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:46.410
You weren't given the most intense or strongest version

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.090
of pain medication that's not necessarily for somebody

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.150
who's just in that situation.

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.650
Last year-- actually it was about two or three years ago, I

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.560
went to an urgent care because I had a bad cough.

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.590
And they tried to prescribe me 100 milligrams of codeine syrup.

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:03.540
I mean, that should never happen.

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:06.030
And I think it's important for people to understand

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.686
that they need to be aware of these things

00:06:08.686 --> 00:06:11.310
and not necessarily take the prescription that's given to them,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:12.755
but ask questions.

00:06:12.755 --> 00:06:15.630
If they are not asking you questions, you need to ask them questions.

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:18.370
You need to make sure that your family member is safe.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:20.730
And if they are taking a prescription, that it's

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:24.360
something that ultimately isn't going to lead them down a long road that

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:27.930
has long-lasting effects for something that

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:31.010
might be an immediate solution that's given.

